.TH CBMAP_BEGIN_ITER 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_begin_iter \- create an iterator positioned at an ordered map's first entry
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cmap_iterator *cbmap_begin_iter(cbmap cbm, char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates an iterator at the first element of the map. Each step gives
the key and the value of one element, which the caller reads through the
unified accessors ccol_iter_key_ptr(3) and ccol_iter_val_ptr(3). The
value pointer points into the storage of the map itself, so a write
through that pointer updates the stored value in place.

The generic ccol_begin(3) macro selects this function for a cbmap and is
the ordinary entry point. Call this function directly only when you
already know the container type and you want the error string.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cbm   Ordered map to iterate over, or NULL
err   Optional pointer that receives an error string on failure, or
      NULL
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to a new iterator at the first element.
Returns NULL and sets *err to NULL when there is nothing to iterate
over, which is the case when cbm is NULL or the map holds no elements.
Returns NULL and sets *err to an error string when the library cannot
allocate the iterator.
.fi
.SH "ITERATION ORDER"
.nf
The walk goes in order over the AVL tree, so it visits the elements in
ascending key order, which is the same order that the map itself uses to
place a key. A hash map gives no such order. This order is part of the
contract of the container, and the caller can depend on it; it is the
reason to choose cbmap instead of chmap when the order matters.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function sets *err to NULL on entry, before anything else, so to
separate an empty result from a failure, test *err instead of the return
value
A NULL cbm iterates as an empty map, and the function reports no error.
This is a deliberate contract across modules, which
chashmap_begin_iter(3) and cvector_begin_iter(3) share: a map field can
still be NULL because the caller put nothing into it yet, and the caller
can iterate such a field directly, without a NULL guard of its own
The iterator frees itself when ccol_iter_next(3) steps past the last
element and returns NULL. A walk that stops early must end with a call to
ccol_iter_destroy(3), unless the caller declares the iterator with
ccol_iter_declare(3), which destroys it at the exit of the scope
The iterator carries a fixed-size stack for the walk, with room for the
maximum height that an AVL tree of any element count can reach, so a new
iterator costs one allocation and each step costs none
The library allocates the iterator through the memory management
procedures of the map
An insert into the map, or a removal from it, during the walk makes the
iterator invalid; a change of a value in place through
ccol_iter_val_ptr(3) does not
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_construct(ages, char *, int);
int age = 30;
cbmap_insert(ages, "alice", age);
age = 41;
cbmap_insert(ages, "bob", age);

char *err = NULL;
ccol_iter_declare(ages, it);
for (it = cbmap_begin_iter(ages, &err); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("%s = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                         *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
if (err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "cannot iterate: %s\en", err);
}
cbmap_destroy(ages);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_iter_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR chashmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cvector_begin_iter (3)
